    "guide": "Identity and Access Management work centers on controlling who can reach which systems and what they can do once they are inside. Day to day that means running identity governance reviews, provisioning and deprovisioning accounts as people join, move, and leave, and building single sign-on and federation so one login carries across an organization's applications. A large share of the postings we reviewed also call out privileged access management, the tighter controls placed on admin and service accounts that can do the most damage if compromised. Readers often confuse this discipline with Cloud and Infrastructure Security, but the two are distinct. Cloud security teams harden servers, networks, and cloud configurations, while IAM teams own the identity layer that decides who is allowed to touch any of it, and several postings in our sample, including roles at Databricks and Cloudflare, sit right at that boundary, pairing IAM duties with cloud platform knowledge.\n\nOur sample does not carry a structured certifications field, so specific certification requirements are drawn only from job titles and named skills rather than a formal credentials list. Titles in the data name concrete specialties employers are hiring for directly, including CIAM work at Deloitte and SailPoint Identity Security Cloud development at Kemper, both signaling that vendor-specific platform knowledge is treated as a real qualification rather than a nice-to-have. NIST appears repeatedly as a named skill tag across postings from Okta, Vercel, GitLab, and others, suggesting familiarity with federal identity frameworks matters for a meaningful slice of these roles. CyberArk does not appear anywhere in our current sample, so we cannot say employers are asking for it right now, and broad claims like CISSP being standard are not supported by what these postings actually list. Where a degree is mentioned it tends to sit at the manager and director level, such as the American Express director roles, though our data does not break out degree requirements as a separate field.\n\nSeniority labels in our sample run from mid-level engineer roles up through staff, principal, manager, and director titles, with senior-level postings the single largest group we pulled. Mid-level openings, like the Non-Human Identity Security Engineer III postings at Edward Jones, tend to focus on hands-on provisioning and governance work inside an established program. Senior and staff roles, which dominate the sample at companies like ServiceNow, Databricks, and CoreWeave, more often own a specific IAM domain end to end, such as identity threat protection or non-human identity. Manager and director postings, including the American Express Director of IAM Engineering and Director of Privileged Access Management roles, sit above the individual contributor track and carry salaries at the top of our recorded range. Because our data does not record required years of experience as its own field, we describe the shape of these roles by seniority label rather than inventing a years figure.\n\nThe skills column in our sample names Okta consistently, appearing on postings from Crusoe, GitLab, Vercel, xAI, and Okta's own listings, making it the most visible named identity platform in our data. SailPoint and Ping Identity show up through job titles and company names rather than the skills tag itself, and Azure, AWS, and GCP all appear regularly, reflecting how much IAM work is tied to a specific cloud provider's identity service. SSO and Zero Trust are the most common access-model terms tagged across the sample, appearing on roles at Lambda, Cloudflare, xAI, and DeepL among others. Protocol names like SAML, OAuth, and SCIM are not broken out as their own tags in our current data, so we note that gap honestly rather than guess which postings require them. Programming and infrastructure skills tagged alongside IAM work include Python, Java, Terraform, and Kubernetes, showing that many of these roles expect engineering ability on top of identity domain knowledge.\n\nAs of today we currently track approximately 51 open Identity and Access Management postings live in our data, out of 96 total records pulled for this review. Of those 96, only 23 carry a stated salary range, and across that smaller group the figures run from $82,600 to $256,250 a year, with Deloitte's CIAM consultant roles anchoring the low end and American Express's director-level postings at the top. That means most postings in our sample do not disclose pay at all, so the range above should be read as a partial picture rather than a market-wide figure. ServiceNow is by far the most frequent employer in our data, followed by Deloitte, then Okta, Databricks, and Elastic, with Edward Jones and Crusoe also appearing more than once. Anyone using this guide should treat the company list as a signal of who is actively hiring in this space right now rather than a ranking of best employers, since our sample reflects postings we happened to pull, not the full labor market.",
